<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uisang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">625–702</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uisang was a Silla Buddhist monk who went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in 661 and studied under Zhiyan, a patriarch of the Huayan school. Returning around 670, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwaeom">Korean Hwaeom</a> school and the temple of Buseoksa in 676. His short verse summary of Huayan teaching became a classic of Korean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He was a friend of the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wonhyo">Wonhyo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uisang.jpg" alt="Uisang" width="240" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%9D%98%EC%83%81%EB%8C%80%EC%82%AC_%EC%98%81%EC%A0%95.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
